CORE
main.cpp
--cpp20
^\[main\.assertion\.1\] .* alignment argument: SUCCESS$
^\[main\.assertion\.2\] .* aligned deallocation: SUCCESS$
^\[main\.assertion\.3\] .* destroying operator delete: SUCCESS$
^\[main\.assertion\.4\] .* sized deallocation: SUCCESS$
^\*\* 1 of \d+ failed
^EXIT=10$
^SIGNAL=0$
--
^warning: ignoring
--
An over-aligned type passes its alignment to the allocation function
([expr.new]/20); the deallocation function of a delete-expression is
selected among the usual ones ([expr.delete]/9): a destroying operator
delete, which runs no destructor, else by alignment, else the unsized one
of a class.  It receives the size or alignment it takes ([expr.delete]/10).
